  "diagnosis": {
    "diagnosis": "BIOMASS_AS_READ",
    "late_biomass_estimate_od": 0.44,
    "p_biomass_above_reading": 0.15,
    "rationale": "The growth curve flattens suddenly at about 0.42 from 6 h onward. That could be a real stationary phase, or it could mean the reader stops responding properly at high density. To tell them apart, I diluted the 18 h aliquot at three factors and multiplied each mean reading back up by its dilution factor. 2x: 0.2324 and 0.2207 → 0.453. 4x: 0.1221 and 0.1112 → 0.467. 10x: 0.0472 and 0.0392 → 0.432. All three diluted readings scale roughly in proportion and agree with the undiluted reading (0.420) to within about 10%, with no trend as dilution increases. If the reader were saturating, the back-calculated values would have climbed well above 0.42 as the sample got more dilute, and they did not. A reader limited near 0.42 would also have given about 0.42 for the 2x and 4x dilutions, not about 0.23 and 0.12. So the plateau is a real stationary phase at OD600 ≈ 0.42–0.47, and the undiluted late readings reflect the actual biomass. The diluted values average slightly higher (about 0.45), but that is within replicate noise. Caveat: I spent the whole budget on the 18 h aliquot, so I'm assuming the earlier plateau hours behave the same way; I didn't measure them."
  },
